     CROSS-PLANE EDUCATION
====================================================== -->

<div
class="panel-card span-4-cols"
style="
background:rgba(255,255,255,0.01);
border-color:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);
margin-top:0.25rem;
">

<div>

<h4
class="card-block-title"
style="
font-size:0.8rem;
text-transform:uppercase;
letter-spacing:0.5px;
color:var(--text-low);
margin-bottom:0.5rem;
">
How the Four Systems Interact
</h4>

</div>


<div class="analysis-footer-grid">


<div class="analysis-block">

<h5>
[E] → [A]
ENVIRONMENT → ATTENTION
</h5>

<p>
A cluttered workspace, constant notifications, multiple open applications, and visible distractions increase the number of competing inputs. Before trying to "increase discipline," reduce unnecessary inputs.
</p>

</div>


<div class="analysis-block">

<h5>
[A] → [B]
ATTENTION → BEHAVIOR
</h5>

<p>
Consistent execution becomes harder when attention repeatedly breaks. Instead of asking only whether a person is motivated, investigate whether the task is receiving uninterrupted attention.
</p>

</div>


<div class="analysis-block">

<h5>
[B] → [L]
BEHAVIOR → LEVERAGE
</h5>

<p>
Repeated actions create information about which processes work. Once a reliable process is identified, ask whether it can be automated, documented, delegated, or converted into a reusable asset.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="deep-technical-matrix">


<div class="audit-list">

<div
style="
font-weight:bold;
font-size:0.75rem;
text-transform:uppercase;
color:var(--text-high);
margin-bottom:0.25rem;
">
Real-World Systems Audit
</div>


<div class="audit-item">

<strong>AUD_01</strong>

Identify the biggest source of attention loss.

</div>


<div class="audit-item">

<strong>AUD_02</strong>

Compare planned behavior with actual behavior.

</div>


<div class="audit-item">

<strong>AUD_03</strong>

Find one repetitive activity that could be simplified or automated.

</div>


<div class="audit-item">

<strong>AUD_04</strong>

Redesign the environment so the desired action requires fewer steps.

</div>

</div>


<div class="remediation-flow">

<div
style="
font-weight:bold;
font-size:0.75rem;
text-transform:uppercase;
color:var(--text-high);
">
Root-Cause Learning Sequence
</div>


<div class="remediation-step">

<h6>
01 // OBSERVE
</h6>

<p>
Do not immediately assume you know why performance changed. Record what actually happened.
</p>

</div>


<div class="remediation-step">

<h6>
02 // DEFINE
</h6>

<p>
Turn vague statements such as "I cannot focus" into observable variables such as interruptions, task changes, elapsed time, or unfinished actions.
</p>

</div>


<div
class="remediation-step"
style="border-color:rgba(6,182,212,0.15);">

<h6 style="color:var(--quantum-cyan);">
03 // EXPERIMENT
</h6>

<p>
Change one variable at a time. For example, remove phone notifications for one work block and compare the result.
</p>

</div>


<div class="remediation-step">

<h6>
04 // LEARN
</h6>

<p>
Keep what works, reject what does not, and document the conditions under which the improvement occurred.
</p>

</div>

</div>

</div>

</div>


